The question I get asked most is how much an AI WhatsApp bot costs. The short answer is that it has two parts: what it costs to **build** it once, and what it costs to **keep it running** every month. The second is usually much lower than people expect.

In this note I explain where each cost comes from, with numbers from two systems I built for businesses in Venezuela, and how to do the math to know if it's worth it for your business.

## What you pay every month

### 1. WhatsApp messages

A serious bot uses the official WhatsApp Business API, not a regular WhatsApp account with a workaround. Meta charges **per message**, and the price depends on the type of message and the customer's country. Venezuela is in the "Rest of Latin America" group.

What matters for a business that serves customers:

- **Replying to a customer who wrote to you is the cheapest part.** When a customer messages you, a 24-hour window opens to reply. Until September 2026 those replies were free; since October 1, 2026 Meta charges each one at the country's utility rate, around **one US cent** in Venezuela. That is why it pays to answer in one complete message instead of three short ones.
- **Messages you start are paid.** A payment reminder or shipping notice ("utility" messages) costs around **one US cent**. A promotion ("marketing" message) costs about **7 cents**.
- Prices change every few months, so check [Meta's official pricing](https://developers.facebook.com/docs/whatsapp/pricing) before doing precise math.

In practice, if your bot mostly **replies** to customers, the WhatsApp bill is very low. What pushes it up is mass campaigns.

### 2. The WhatsApp platform

You can connect directly to Meta's API or use a provider (such as Callbell or others) that gives you a shared inbox so your team can see the chats. Connecting directly has no monthly fee; a provider charges a monthly plan, but saves you from building that inbox. It depends on whether your team needs to see and take over conversations.

### 3. The AI

It's the cost that scares people most, and it's usually the smallest. A real data point: in the [payments automation case](/en/notes/automatizar-pagos-whatsapp-12-a-3-personas), the company processes about **72,000 operations a month** and pays **about US$150 a month in AI**. That includes reading transfer screenshots, understanding messages and replying.

The cost depends on how many messages the bot processes, whether it reads images or voice notes, and which model it uses. A good system measures that spend every month so there are no surprises.

### 4. The server

The bot lives on a server that receives messages, queries your systems and stores conversations. For most businesses a modest server is enough, in the range of **tens of dollars a month**, not hundreds.

### 5. Maintenance

Systems change: your catalog grows, you switch banks, Meta updates its API, new questions come up. A bot nobody maintains degrades. Plan for monthly maintenance or support hours when needed.

## What it costs to build

There's no honest list price here, because it depends on what the bot has to do. A bot that answers FAQs doesn't cost the same as one that:

- Checks your real inventory in Shopify, Odoo or your own system.
- Validates payments against the bank, including Pago Móvil.
- Converts prices to bolívares at the day's rate.
- Hands the conversation to a person when needed.
- Comes with a dashboard so your team can see orders, payments and chats.

What moves the price most is **how many systems it connects to** and **how sensitive its work is**. A bot that touches money needs more checks than one that only gives information.

That's why I prefer to look at your case and give you a fixed price, instead of a range that doesn't help you decide.

## The math that actually matters

The useful question isn't “how much does the bot cost?”, but “how much does it cost me **not** to have it?”. Two real examples:

- **A store in Caracas** had about 10 people answering chat, and customers waited more than 7 hours on average, sometimes a week. With the bot, the first reply arrives in about 2 minutes and the chat team went down to 2 or 3 people: **more than US$2,100 a month** in payroll. [Read the full case](/en/notes/bot-whatsapp-7-horas-a-2-minutos).
- **A company that collects payments on WhatsApp** went from 12 people in operations to 3, with about US$150 a month in AI. It saves **about US$2,700 a month** in payroll and serves twice as many customers. [Read the full case](/en/notes/automatizar-pagos-whatsapp-12-a-3-personas).

To do your own math:

1. **How many people** spend the day answering the same questions or validating payments, and what they cost you each month.
2. **How long** it takes you to reply today, and how many sales you lose because of it.
3. **How much** it would cost to keep the bot running each month: WhatsApp, AI, server and maintenance.

If item 1 is much bigger than item 3, the bot pays for itself in a few months.

## When it's not worth it

A bot isn't always the answer. It's probably not worth it if:

- You get few messages a day and one person handles them fine.
- Every conversation is different and needs human judgment from start to finish.
- The data the bot would need (inventory, prices, orders) isn't in any system.

In those cases, what you need first is sometimes to organize the process or build an internal dashboard, before thinking about a bot.

